" (https://onlinejudge.org/external/12/1261.pdf)\nciteturn10017view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 1; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://onlinejudge.org/external/12/1261.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 32\nL0@P0: 1261 String Popping\nL1@P0: We are given a string s of two characters ‘a’ and ‘b’. Let a group be a maximal consecutive substring\nL2@P0: of the same character. Any group g of s of length at least two can be removed (or popped) and a new\nL3@P0: string is constructed by concatenating the remaining left and right substrings of s. We repeat this\nL4@P0: process until either the string becomes the empty string or there is no more group of length at least\nL5@P0: two.\nL6@P0: For example, string s = babbbaaabb has 5 groups b, a, bbb, aaa, and bb. The string s can be turned\nL7@P0: into the empty string by popping groups in the following sequence (the underlined group is to be popped\nL8@P0: in the sequence):\nL9@P0: babbbaaabb → baaaabb → bbb → empty string\nL10@P0: But the group may not turn to an empty string by a different sequence of pop operations:\nL11@P0: babbbaaabb → babbbaaa → baaaa → b\nL12@P0: Given a string, write a program to decide whether the string can be turned into the empty string\nL13@P0: by some sequence of popping operations.\nL14@P0: Input\nL15@P0: Your program is to read from standard input. The input consists of T test cases. The number of test\nL16@P0: cases T is given in the first line of the input. Each test case consists of a single line containing a string\nL17@P0: of two characters ‘a’ and ‘b’. The minimum and maximum length of the string is 1 and 25, respectively.\nL18@P0: Output\nL19@P0: Your program is to write to standard output. Print one line for each test case. The line of each test\nL20@P0: case should contain ‘0’ or 1’. Print 1’ if the input string can be turned to an empty string by a sequence\nL21@P0: of popping operations. Otherwise print ‘0’.\nL22@P0: The following shows sample input and output for three test cases.\nL23@P0: Sample Input\nL24@P0: 3\nL25@P0: babbbaaabb\nL26@P0: aabbaabb\nL27@P0: abab\nL28@P0: Sample Output\nL29@P0: 1\nL30@P0: 1\nL31@P0: 0\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCandy Crush - LeetCode (https://leetcode.com/problems/candy-crush/description/)\nciteturn10017view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://leetcode.com/problems/candy-crush/description/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 35\nL0:   * cite0†Log in L1: \nL2: Description\nL3: \nL4: Description\nL5: \nL6: Editorial\nL7: \nL8: Editorial\nL9: \nL10: Solutions\nL11: \nL12: Solutions\nL13: \nL14: Submissions\nL15: \nL16: Submissions\nL17: \nL18: Code\nL19: \nL20: Code\nL21: \nL22: Testcase\nL23: \nL24: Testcase\nL25: \nL26: Test Result\nL27: \nL28: Test Result\nL29: \nL30: Subscribe to unlock.\nL31: \nL32: Thanks for using LeetCode! To view this question you must subscribe to premium.\nL33: \nL34: cite1†Subscribe --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCandy crush on a single dimensional array. · GitHub (https://gist.github.com/kbecker42/a85b1febb9bac94d87c476ded32010fe)\nciteturn10017view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.1 years ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://gist.github.com/kbecker42/a85b1febb9bac94d87c476ded32010fe\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 188\nL0: Skip to content\nL1: \nL2: cite0†  L3: \nL4: Search Gists\nL5: \nL6:   Search Gists\nL7: \nL8: cite1†All gists cite2†Back to GitHub†github.com cite3† Sign in  cite4† Sign up  L9: \nL10: cite0†  L11: \nL12: cite3† Sign in  cite4† Sign up  L13: \nL14: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session.   Dismiss alert\nL15: \nL16: {{ message }}\nL17: \nL18: Instantly share code, notes, and snippets.\nL19: \nL20: cite24†Image: @kbecker42†gist.github.com L21: #  cite5†kbecker42 /cite6†candyCrush.js L22: \nL23: Last active May 21, 2021 18:25\nL24: \nL25:   Show Gist options\nL26: \nL27:   * cite7† Download ZIP  L28: \nL29:   * cite8† Star 0 ( 0 )  You must be signed in to star a gist\nL30:   * cite8† Fork 0 ( 0 )  You must be signed in to fork a gist\nL31: \nL32:   *   Embed\nL33: \nL34:     *   Embed  Embed this gist in your website.\nL35:     *   Share  Copy sharable link for this gist.\nL36:     *   Clone via HTTPS  Clone using the web URL.\nL37:     *     * cite9† Learn more about clone URLs †docs.github.com L38: Clone this repository at &lt;script src=&quot;https://gist.github.com/kbecker42/a85b1febb9bac94d87c476ded32010fe.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\nL39: \nL40:   *   Save kbecker42/a85b1febb9bac94d87c476ded32010fe to your computer and use it in GitHub Desktop.\nL41: \nL42: cite6† Code  cite10† Revisions 5  L43: \nL44:   Embed\nL45: \nL46:   *   Embed  Embed this gist in your website.\nL47:   *   Share  Copy sharable link for this gist.\nL48:   *   Clone via HTTPS  Clone using the web URL.\nL49:   *   * cite9† Learn more about clone URLs †docs.github.com L50: Clone this repository at &lt;script src=&quot;https://gist.github.com/kbecker42/a85b1febb9bac94d87c476ded32010fe.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\nL51: \nL52:   Save kbecker42/a85b1febb9bac94d87c476ded32010fe to your computer and use it in GitHub Desktop.\nL53: \nL54: cite7†Download ZIP L55: \nL56: Candy crush on a single dimensional array.\nL57: \nL58: cite11† Raw  L59: \nL60: cite12† candyCrush.js  L61: This file contains hidden or bidirectional Unicode text that may be interpreted or compiled differently than what appears below. To review, open the file in an editor that reveals hidden Unicode characters. Learn more about bidirectional Unicode characters\nL62: \nL63: Show hidden characters\nL64: /* Write a function to crush candy in one dimensional board. In candy crushing games, groups of like items are removed from the board. In this problem, any sequence of 3 or more like items should be removed and any items adjacent to that sequence should now be considered adjacent to each other. This process should be repeated as many time as possible. You should greedily remove characters from left to right.\nL65: See also https://leetcode.com/discuss/interview-question/380650/Bloomberg-or-Phone-Screen-or-Candy-Crush-1D\nL66: Input: \"aaabbbc\"\nL67: Output: \"c\"\nL68: Explanation:\nL69: 1. Remove 3 'a': \"aaabbbbc\" => \"bbbbc\"\nL70: 2. Remove 4 'b': \"bbbbc\" => \"c\"\nL71: */\nL72: \nL73: const candyCrush = input => {\nL74:  let count = 0;\nL75:  let lastChar = input[0];\nL76:  let retry = true;\nL77:  let chars = input.split('');\nL78: \nL79:  while (retry) {\nL80:  const list = [];\nL81:  retry = false;\nL82:  chars.forEach(char => {\nL83:  if (lastChar !== char) {\nL84:  // We have arrived at a different character.\nL85:  if (count >= 3) {\nL86:  // Match, so remove this character.\nL87:  retry = true;\nL88:  }\nL89:  else {\nL90:  // No match, so keep it and push it back onto the list.\nL91:  for (let i=0; i<count; i++) {\nL92:  list.push(lastChar);\nL93:  }\nL94:  }\nL95: \nL96:  // Reset counter.\nL97:  count = 0;\nL98:  lastChar = char;\nL99:  }\nL100: \nL101:  count++;\nL102:  });\nL103: \nL104:  if (count < 3) {\nL105:  list.push(lastChar);\nL106:  }\nL107:  chars = list;\nL108:  count = 0;\nL109:  lastChar = chars[0];\nL110:  }\nL111: \nL112:  return chars;\nL113: }\nL114: \nL115: cite13† Raw  L116: \nL117: cite14† candyCrush2.js  L118: \nL119: This file contains hidden or bidirectional Unicode text that may be interpreted or compiled differently than what appears below. To review, open the file in an editor that reveals hidden Unicode characters. Learn more about bidirectional Unicode characters\nL120: \nL121: Show hidden characters\nL122: \nL123: candyCrush = str => {\nL124:  let result = '';\nL125:  let lastIndex = 0;\nL126:  let retry = false;\nL127:  for (let i=lastIndex; i<str.length; i++) {\nL128:  const char = str[i];\nL129: \nL130:  if (char !== str[lastIndex]) {\nL131:  // We have arrived at a different character.\nL132:  const distance = i - lastIndex;\nL133:  if (distance >= 3) {\nL134:  // Match, so remove all letters from lastIndex to here (exclusive).\nL135:  retry = true;\nL136:  }\nL137:  else {\nL138:  // No match, include the preceeding letters.\nL139:  result += str.substr(lastIndex, distance);\nL140:  }\nL141: \nL142:  // Move to the current letter for matches.\nL143:  lastIndex = i;\nL144:  }\nL145:  }\nL146:  if (str.length - lastIndex < 3) {\nL147:  // Including trailing letters.\nL148:  result += str.substr(lastIndex);\nL149:  }\nL150: \nL151:  if (retry) {\nL152:  result = candyCrush(result);\nL153:  }\nL154: \nL155:  return result;\nL156: };\nL157: \nL158: cite15† Raw  L159: \nL160: cite16† test.js  L161: \nL162: This file contains hidden or bidirectional Unicode text that may be interpreted or compiled differently than what appears below. To review, open the file in an editor that reveals hidden Unicode characters. Learn more about bidirectional Unicode characters\nL163: \nL164: Show hidden characters\nL165: console.log(candyCrush('aaabbbc')); // c\nL166: console.log(candyCrush('aabbbacd')); // cd\nL167: console.log(candyCrush('aabbccddeeedcba')); // []\nL168: console.log(candyCrush('aaabbbacd')); // acd\nL169: console.log(candyCrush('dddabbbbaccccaax')); // x\nL170: \nL171: cite17†Sign up for free to join this conversation on GitHub. Already have an account? cite8†Sign in to comment L172: ## Footer\nL173: \nL174: cite2† †github.com © 2025 GitHub, Inc.\nL175: \nL176: ### Footer navigation\nL177: \nL178:   * cite18†Terms†docs.github.com L179:   * cite19†Privacy†docs.github.com L180:   * cite20†Security†github.com L181:   * cite21†Status†ww